{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792334953,"nonce":"+VVSLzVIOJkhxlbQNUSEklD+qs0=","title":"The page agent drops a press, a release or the redraw request when the program stops reading","message":"**What happens**\nThe agent posts each event it sees to a ring in the program's memory (64 KB, about a thousand records). A program that reads nothing for a while, as in a long collection or a shader build, lets the ring fill, and the agent drops, and counts, each record after that. It does not tell the kinds apart: the newest pointer positions are kept no better than a button release. Measured in headless Chrome with the program's thread frozen by the debugger and three thousand moves, a release and twenty wheel turns sent: about fifteen hundred records dropped, the release and the wheel among them. A program that comes back to a lost release sees the button held until the next click, so a drag in a scene stays on.\n\nThe agent also cleared the redraw word before it posted the redraw, so a dropped redraw was never asked for again; a program that paces its frames on it waits out its own fallback, a second.\n\n**Done**\nThe ring is 256 KB. Once it is three quarters full the agent holds, rather than posts, the events that only say where a pointer is or add to a wheel or a pinch: the newest position, the sums of the rest. They go in when the program has read some, and before any other event, so a press lands where the pointer was. Every other event has the whole ring. The redraw word is taken only once the redraw is posted. A record lost anyway is said on the console, once in two seconds. tests/page_agent.rs floods the agent and checks that the press, the release, the last position, the summed wheel and the redraw arrive.\n\n**Not done**\nThe program does not learn that records were dropped, apart from the console. A resynchronising event could say so.","files":null}]}